/// Matplotlib-style plotting for Koala's ML stack (`koala/plot`).
///
/// The package is split into a pure-Koala **spec/geometry layer** and a
/// thin **renderer**. All layout math lives Koala-side: nice-ticks, axis
/// margins, data->pixel transforms, legend and subplot layout, histogram
/// binning, colormaps and contour marching squares are resolved here, and
/// the figure spec carries *resolved pixel geometry*. The renderer is a
/// dumb painter (a self-written single-file canvas-2D script over the
/// system webview) that only strokes what the spec already computed. This
/// keeps the geometry headless-testable and cheap to reuse for future
/// LVGL / `koala/ui` backends.
///
/// v1 holds data as `list[float]` / `list[list[float]]` (rendering geometry
/// is not ML numeric computation; see docs/koala_ml.md §4.15). A builtin
/// `Tensor` entry will be added later without reworking the geometry layer.
/// The ML computation path itself stays Tensor-only.
///
/// User-facing API mirrors a `matplotlib.pyplot` subset, so a matplotlib
/// script migrates with near-zero learning cost:
///
///     import "koala/plot" as plt
///
///     func main() {
///         plt.plot([0.0, 1.0, 2.0], [0.0, 1.0, 4.0], "ro--", label = "y = x^2")
///         plt.xlabel("x")
///         plt.title("demo")
///         plt.legend()
///         plt.show()          // blocks until the window is closed
///     }
///
/// v1 surface: `figure(figsize=(w,h), dpi)` (inches, matplotlib convention),
/// `subplot` / `subplots`, `plot` (incl. fmt strings like "ro--"), `scatter`
/// (`s`/`c`), `contour`, `imshow`, `hist` (returns `(n, bins, patches)`),
/// `xlabel/ylabel/title/legend/xlim/ylim/grid/xticks/yticks`, `gcf/gca`,
/// `show`, `close`, plus the Koala extension `savehtml(path)`. The OO
/// `Figure`/`Axes` objects use matplotlib's `set_*` method names.
///
/// Koala-forced deviations (static typing / reserved words), documented at
/// each call site: `plot(y, fmt)` is `plot(y, fmt = "...")`; `hist(range=)`
/// is `hist(range_=)`; `subplots()` returns `(fig, first_ax)` with the full
/// grid on `fig.axes`; `show()` blocks by default (matplotlib does not).
///
/// Output backend is chosen by `KOALA_PLOT_BACKEND`: `webview` (default)
/// opens a system-webview window; `html` writes `plot.html` headlessly.
/// `savehtml(path)` always writes a self-contained HTML file (the
/// display-less exit; `savefig` is deferred, docs/koala_ml.md §6.5).
///
/// The geometry primitives (`nice_ticks`, `Scale`, `autoscale`, ...) are
/// public so the spec layer can be driven headlessly in unit tests, the
/// same convention `koala/ui` uses for `dispatch` / `pump_timers`. Most
/// application code reaches them through `Figure` / `Axes` / pyplot.